# Copy to .env beside docker-compose.yml. Never commit the real one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- required

# Host directory for repositories, worktrees and the database.
#
# A host path rather than a named volume, and mounted at the SAME path inside the
# containers. The T0 sandbox asks the host daemon to bind-mount a worktree into a
# sibling container by absolute path, and the daemon resolves it on the host — a
# named volume would mount an empty directory and tests would report clean for
# code they never saw.
LORE_HOST_DATA=/var/lib/lore

# The uid/gid owning LORE_HOST_DATA. The container runs as this, so the database
# and worktrees it writes are owned by you rather than by a container-internal
# user. `id -u` / `id -g`.
LORE_UID=1000
LORE_GID=1000

# Address the service listens on. Loopback by default, deliberately.
#
# The design assumed a tailnet, where WireGuard is the perimeter. If this host has
# no tailscale, then on a LAN THE BEARER TOKENS ARE THE PERIMETER — set this to the
# LAN address knowing that, or put the host on a tailnet first.
LORE_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Z.AI Coding Plan (the subscription). One key, read by opencode directly — no
# interactive login is needed on a headless host.
#
# NOTE the provider id: `zai-coding-plan` hits api.z.ai/api/CODING/paas/v4, the
# subscription endpoint. Plain `zai` hits .../paas/v4 and bills per token from the
# same key, so the id in LORE_TIERS is what decides which one you are paying on.
ZHIPU_API_KEY=

# Which ladder to run.
#
# `tiers.zai-kimi-openai.json` is THREE VENDORS on three subscriptions, no metered API:
#
#   t1  zai-coding-plan/glm-5-turbo  medium  the gate, where nearly all volume lands
#   t2  kimi-for-coding/k3           high    an independent vendor for the first deep look
#   t3  openai/gpt-5.6-terra         high    ChatGPT Plus, via opencode's OAuth login
#
# One vendor per tier is the point (D-32, D-49). Until 2026-08-06 t1 and t2 were both
# Z.ai, so two thirds of the ladder shared a blind spot — not falsely reported, since
# the sole-vendor check only fires when EVERY tier is one vendor, but thinner
# independence than the ladder implies.
#
# `k3`, not `k3-256k`: the suffix names the SMALLER variant (262,144 against
# 1,048,576), and this deployment has already sent 204,609 tokens in one review.
#
# `tiers.zai-openai.json` is the previous two-vendor ladder, kept as a fallback for a
# deployment without a Kimi subscription.
#
# OpenAI sits at T3 ON PURPOSE, for two reasons that happen to agree:
#
#   * ChatGPT Plus is the entry tier and its usage is capped on a rolling window.
#     T3 runs only after both cheaper tiers came back clean, so it is the rarest
#     call in the ladder — the one place a capped subscription fits.
#   * The last line of defence is exactly where an independent vendor is worth
#     most. A blind spot shared with T1 and T2 would be invisible precisely at the
#     moment nothing else is left to catch it.
#
# There is deliberately NO single-vendor tier file in the repo any more. One existed,
# every review flagged it, and the flag was right: a config whose only purpose is to
# be the worse option is a liability that someone eventually points LORE_TIERS at.
# D-49 already handles the case generically — a ladder whose reachable tiers share one
# vendor reaches `passed_partial` at best, never `passed`, whoever wrote the file.
# A deployment with one subscription writes its own, and gets told what it costs.
#
# The OpenAI credential is OAUTH, not an API key: it carries `expires` and `refresh`,
# so the container needs auth.json WRITABLE or the token cannot be renewed. An API
# key never needed to write; this does.
#
# THE CONTAINER'S OWN PATH, not the host's — found by lore's own review, fingerprints
# 15be66bd/1be9520f: this named `/opt/lore/...`, a path that exists nowhere in the
# image (Dockerfile: WORKDIR /app, COPY deploy/tiers.*.json ./deploy/ — the files
# live at /app/deploy/). loadTiers() reads this as a file path and throws ENOENT,
# unhandled, at boot — service/main.ts's fallback-check IIFE calls it inside a
# discarded `void (async () => {...})()` with no `.catch`, so the rejection crashes
# the whole process on every restart. The recommended three-vendor ladder above,
# not the two-vendor fallback: the comment already frames tiers.zai-openai.json as
# "kept as a fallback for a deployment without a Kimi subscription", so pointing the
# default at the OTHER file contradicted the very comment beside it.
LORE_TIERS=/app/deploy/tiers.zai-kimi-openai.json

# THE FALLBACK CREDENTIAL, not the primary ladder — found by lore's own review,
# fingerprint dba7d45f: this used to describe the superseded D-17 design ("fronts
# all three model tiers with one key... the token spend is cheaper anyway"), which
# is not what either tiers file above does. Every route in both is a subscription;
# `openrouter/...` appears ONLY inside `fallback` arrays, asked for real just when a
# subscription is out of quota — and it bills per call (README: "~$4.83 a call" in
# one incident, "$101.36 in one morning" in another). LORE_ALLOW_METERED (default 0,
# see docker-compose.yml) gates whether a fallback may ever reach it at all; leaving
# this key unset is a legitimate, supported choice — an outage then costs coverage
# (a tier skipped, `passed_partial`) rather than money, D-48's existing path.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# THE SECOND Z.AI SEAT — found by lore's own review, fingerprint 366453ed:
# tiers.zai-kimi-openai.json (the ladder LORE_TIERS above now defaults to) names
# `zai-coding-plan2/glm-5.2` as its shared `helper` and as t1/t2/t3's own first
# fallback, and docker-compose.yml reads this exact variable. Left unset, every one
# of those routes fails authentication — the second-plan spares the ladder exists to
# provide are dead from the first quota outage, with nothing at preflight saying so
# (it checks OPENROUTER_API_KEY / ZHIPU_API_KEY, never this one). A second key on
# the same Z.AI Coding Plan subscription, distinct from ZHIPU_API_KEY above.
ZAI2_API_KEY=

# Basic auth between lore and opencode. Both services read these; a mismatch
# produces a bare 401 with no explanation.
OPENCODE_SERVER_USERNAME=lore
OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD=

# Test execution is GONE (D-71), so there is nothing here to switch on. lore reads a
# test suite — whether the change is covered, whether a test asserts what its name
# claims — and never runs one. A failing suite belongs to whoever owns the repository,
# and their CI already tells them.
#
# The sandbox is still needed: `tsc` and `eslint` resolve through the target's
# node_modules, so the install runs, and installs run lifecycle scripts with network.

# ------------------------------------------------------- strongly advised

# Where the knowledge base is replicated to, on THIS machine.
#
# Replication is always on and needs no credentials: litestream writes a
# continuously-restorable copy into this folder, and an OUTER SCRIPT carries the
# files off the machine. lore does not know or care how that happens, which is the
# whole point of the split — the half it can be responsible for, it does properly.
#
# Which also means this alone is not a backup. A copy on this disk survives a
# corrupted database, a bad bulk write and a wrong `down-hard`; it does not survive
# the disk. `make backup-check` reports the local half only. `make backup-drill`
# proves a restore actually works, on a copy, without touching the live database.
LORE_HOST_BACKUP=./backup

# Plane MCP. The opencode config references these BY NAME rather than embedding
# the key, which is why that config file is safe to copy between machines while
# the secret stays here. Leave empty to run without Plane.
PLANE_API_KEY=
PLANE_WORKSPACE_SLUG=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- optional

# Where devops alerts go. Any endpoint that accepts a JSON POST: Slack,
# Alertmanager, a Plane integration, a shell script behind a tiny HTTP server.
#
# LEFT BLANK, this deployment is monitored by nobody — found by lore's own review,
# fingerprint ac45b54e: unlike PLANE_API_KEY above ("leave empty to run without
# Plane"), nothing here said that, and preflight (deploy/Makefile) checks only the
# opencode password, never either alerting variable. Blank is a legitimate choice —
# alerts still reach the container log — but it should be a choice, not a default
# nobody meant to make.
LORE_WEBHOOK_URL=

# The deadman. Whatever consumes this MUST alert when the beat STOPS — that is the
# whole point. Push-only alerting cannot detect its own death, so silence is the
# signal that survives a broken alerter, a full disk and a dead network alike.
#
# LEFT BLANK, same as above: nothing pages if `lore` itself stops running, and this
# is the one alert that survives lore being dead — spec/operations.md §3 calls it
# "the difference between monitoring and hoping."
LORE_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# May a review fall back onto a route that bills per call (D-117)? "0" is the
# default: every tier above has a subscription route, and a tier whose plan is out
# is honestly reported as skipped (`passed_partial`) rather than quietly bought at
# ~$4.83 a call. Set to "1" to buy the coverage instead — a deliberate purchase, not
# a fallback. Named here so the choice is discoverable from this file alone —
# found by lore's own review, fingerprint 3297759e's own root cause: an operator
# had no way to learn this variable exists without already reading
# docker-compose.yml or config-view.ts's live /config.json.
LORE_ALLOW_METERED=0
